### Auth: login, session, refresh rotation, reuse detection, logout
### Mirrors tests/integration/test_auth_flows.py
### Send the login request first — the rest reuse {{login.response.body.*}}.

@baseUrl = http://localhost:8000

### Log in — returns a short-lived access JWT and an opaque refresh token (starts a token family)
# @name login
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/auth/login
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "email": "admin@aurora-clinic.com",
  "password": "password123"
}

### Who am I — use the access token
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/auth/me
Authorization: Bearer {{login.response.body.access_token}}

### Refresh — consumes the presented refresh token and issues a NEW one in the same family
# @name refresh
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/auth/refresh
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "refresh_token": "{{login.response.body.refresh_token}}"
}

### Reuse detection — present the ORIGINAL (now-consumed) refresh token again.
### Expect 401: the whole family is revoked, because a replayed token is the classic sign of theft.
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/auth/refresh
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "refresh_token": "{{login.response.body.refresh_token}}"
}

### After reuse detection, even the freshly-issued token is dead (family revoked). Expect 401.
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/auth/refresh
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "refresh_token": "{{refresh.response.body.refresh_token}}"
}

### Logout — revoke the token's whole family (idempotent)
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/auth/logout
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "refresh_token": "{{login.response.body.refresh_token}}"
}

### No token — expect 401 problem+json
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/auth/me
